orytellers to  to bring something new to the table you  know for me it felt like  while the primary objective was to be  faithful to the mythology as much as we  possibly could  and to the lord we also wanted to make  sure that we weren't just retelling the  same  thing that had been told 100 times  before that you know this was never  going to be a remake  of the old film this was a reboot so for  that  it was what is you know how are we going  to add something new to the story  uh i you know i don't there was never  any there were never any drafts where  liu kang or sonia  or johnny cage were the main characters  and  what i'll say is i don't i've always  looked at this  as an ensemble you know i always think  that while we have a point of view in  cole young that brings us into that  world and that's part of the the reason  for his his being is that he allows  he allows the audience to kind of  venture into this very dense  mythological world  and allows us to get through a lot of  what we have to tell the audience  uh in a more organic way um you know it  was never that he was going to become  like the the poster child and and take  over the whole thing it was always that  uh it would become an ensemble and all  those kind of iconic classic characters  would kind of be unlocked along your  journey  so that by the end um you'd have they'd  all get their little moments to shine in  their screen time and then  at the end you kind of end up in this  justice league avengers style  ensemble group so um you know that was  part of it and it was trying to be  original trying to bring something new  to the table trying to  uh give the audience away into the movie  and also uh  part of that part of my job in bringing  a new character in was making sure that  if we were going to do that that he felt  like he felt part of that universe that  he was tied into this story that he was  tied into the characters in a way  that uh allowed him to live and breathe  in that world without feeling like he  was this kind of fish out of water just  thrown in from it for no reason  yeah that makes sense uh you brought up  the original 90s film  um full disclosure i am uh  i get like kind of made fun of in the  collider slack i'm a huge fan of the 90s  mortal kombat film ashamed of my friend  what's that nothing to be ashamed of  that's well i appreciate that i'll  thank you um i didn't have anything to  do why am i thanking you  um i'm curious what  were elements of that film that you were  maybe trying to purposely  borrow from and what were you purposely  trying to avoid from that film yeah no  that's a that's a good question so i  love the original two  uh people that made this movie love the  original they made the movie right so  this is the studio  in 1995 and uh and you know  we love it and and so part of our  respect for it was that we wanted to  make sure that we weren't just carbon  copying their story because that movie  exists  and yeah you know i felt like it would  be doing the brand and that film and  disservice if we just try to  stick too closely to it so one thing we  wanted to avoid was just we didn't want  to tell the same story  um plus since in the 25 years mortal  kombat has expanded its story lines to  incredible places um  you know if you go back and watch the  original scorpion and sub-zero they  don't they they're silent  antagonists they don't do anything other  than kind of have a couple fight scenes  and that's it so  those characters have grown  exponentially in mortal kombat over the  years and i've  arguably become the poster children of  the franchise now so  we wanted to lean into that for our  adaptation really give the fans the  scorpion subzero stuff that they never  got in 1995.  um i wanted to stick closely i think  everyone wanted to stick closely to the  tone too we loved  kind of that wink wink kind of  cheesiness  of the the 95 film it's part of what  made it  made it great it's part of what we  remember it