o's arc i  knew that i knew that it was it was  important  the the trick with any of these things  is how do you tell a story that  you know that takes place back then  but that also matters to today because  we wanted we didn't want this film to  take place in the past so  we wanted to have a modern uh through  line to it so  it was it was trying to figure out okay  if i've got this very emotional  uh setup and and and i'll just speak to  that because  you know when you when you're adapting  when you're writer you're adapting  something like mortal kombat i'm trying  to find a motion anywhere i can because  you know this isn't uh you know it's not  this profoundly  deep emotional uh ip you know it's it's  so so when i looked at it and i said  where can i pull out the emotion  i looked at scorpion's backstory right  and it is tragic and it's it's probably  that  probably the most tragic thing in all in  in the whole ip so  i said i want that to be part of it i  think that needs to be part of it i  think we need to start there  and then it was a matter of okay if i'm  going to start there then it has to  matter later so how do i find a way to  bring it back  to the story so that it has an emotional  payoff at the end of the story as well  so then i decided i was going to find a  way to bookend that chapter you know  scorpion sub-zero at the beginning  and at the end but but not just have  them be isolated uh  sequences that throughout the the meat  of the movie you're tracking that story  and and then that's where cole really  became an essential part of my story is  i knew i could use him  to bridge the uh the scorpion sub-zero  storyline with the modern day storyline  and without giving you know anything  away there it's it's that that then  then i started to see okay i can see a  path i can see a way to  to uh connect these dots throughout the  journey  let's touch on another kind of iconic  pair in the film  but this one i was intrigued by how it  was a little bit deconstructed from how  we normally see them  um sonia and kano are sort of  like tenuous allies in this take on the  material  i don't know about that all right we'll  say tenuous tenuous they you know the  enemy of my enemy  uh and i'm curious where that decis they  almost gave me like  you know gritty buddy cop comedy  like kind of vibes where did the  decision to sort of put them together  come from yeah so it's a so it's a  narrative decision right so  so basically the idea is when you're  when you're constructing your  story uh you know let's say i've got  three protagonists  let's let's actually let's mix it up and  say that the movie was sonia  uh liu kang and cole so there's three  heroic characters that are going on  their journey  and they're trying to figure out what's  going on but they all have one unified  pov they all have one perspective which  is  we gotta go figure this out we gotta  beat the bad guys well when you write  that and you start to see that  it starts to feel very one-sided and so  what i always like to do as a writer is  i always try to find conflict wherever i  can because conflict drives narrative  and conflict drives character so  for me it was how do i add conflict into  the mix  of those protagonists journey and the  best way to do it is to drop somebody in  there like a wild  you know fox and a chicken pen and  that's kano and he's a guy that  uh has his own self-serving egotistical  [ __ ] desires and he's going to pursue  them no matter what  and what that does is it immediately  gives me conflict it allows him to  be a problem and a pain in the ass for  our heroes as they go on their journey  and it's just it's always more of a it's  always more of a  rewarding uh setup to have for your  characters is to have conflict like that  so  that was the idea of putting kano in the  mix and then  uh and then josh uh added so much to it  he's you know he's he's he's a great  actor and brought a lot to  their you know so says jess and